WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.296 --> 00:00:01.810
- [Female Voiceover] Many
people know the sound of vinyl,

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.724
but it's how it's made that's
a whole different story.

00:00:04.724 --> 00:00:05.557
- [Ton Voiceover] The
record industry offers

00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.381
a one-stop-shop for all our customers,

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:11.748
so we do the cuts in-house
on both lacquer and DMM.

00:00:11.748 --> 00:00:13.599
We produce our own coppers.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.212
We do the print of all
the sleeves, labels,

00:00:17.212 --> 00:00:19.814
so the customer doesn't
have to go to other printers

00:00:19.814 --> 00:00:22.904
or cutting rooms to get
all their stuff together.

00:00:22.904 --> 00:00:24.738
We do everything in-house.

00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:27.459
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:29.959
(bell chimes)

00:00:31.348 --> 00:00:33.168
- [Female Voiceover] This
is Ton, the proud owner

00:00:33.168 --> 00:00:35.025
of Record Industry, one of the largest

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:38.131
vinyl pressing plants in the world.

00:00:38.131 --> 00:00:39.907
Located in Haarlem in the Netherlands,

00:00:39.907 --> 00:00:43.189
Ton has had a lifelong love of vinyl.

00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:46.515
- I started this, my music
career, as being a DJ

00:00:46.515 --> 00:00:49.347
in a local club, private radio stations

00:00:49.347 --> 00:00:52.852
playing, uh, the old disco
dance grooves in the '80s.

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.699
- [Female Voiceover] The
factory opened in 1958

00:00:54.699 --> 00:00:57.208
and in recent years has
seen a surge in sales.

00:00:57.208 --> 00:00:59.499
- Last year, we did nine and a half million,

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:02.903
and this year we're aiming for 10,500,000,

00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:03.860
maybe 11,000,000.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.997
(upbeat music)

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:07.476
We press for all the major companies

00:01:07.476 --> 00:01:10.733
being Sony Music, Universal, Warner Music,

00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:13.603
and a lot of local smaller labels.

00:01:13.603 --> 00:01:15.571
My wife and me try to run Record Industry

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:17.250
as being a family company.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.828
We don't have children ourselves,

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:20.719
but in the end we're
always making a bit fun

00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:24.302
of it and we say we
have 100 children here.

00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:26.465
I like the vinyl.

00:01:26.465 --> 00:01:28.216
I like the size of the format,

00:01:28.216 --> 00:01:30.218
nice pictures, you know?

00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:32.864
You can say you can be
proud of this product.

00:01:32.864 --> 00:01:35.735
If you look at a CD, for instance,

00:01:35.735 --> 00:01:37.875
it's not something you're cherishing,

00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:39.437
a record you cherish.

00:01:39.437 --> 00:01:41.701
The music was more some kind of a hobby,

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.388
so I could turn my hobby
into a real profession

00:01:44.388 --> 00:01:46.032
running this beautiful plant.

00:01:46.032 --> 00:01:48.615
(upbeat music)

00:01:51.354 --> 00:01:53.854
(bell chimes)

